The trained $\dt$-embedding triggers CIVO onset (NVE $\geq 2.0\times$) in 3 of 5 Walker Walk seeds, and universally degrades performance across all five to below 30\% of baseline (peak NVE $1.53$--$4.72\times$; IQM $148.8$, $n{=}5$; \citet{agarwal2021precipice}). Including replications and Cheetah Run (\S\ref{sec:f6}), CIVO onset occurs in 5 of 7 tested multi-joint locomotion runs (71\%, two environments). Of the 5 onset runs, 3 exhibit sustained collapse, one self-corrects, and one shows NVE self-correction without policy recovery (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).

\paragraph{Mechanism evidence.}
On Walker Walk, the RSSM reward head develops a $1.63\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ under trained conditioning (reward MAE $0.053 \to 0.087$; Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}), while observation reconstruction improves (gap $1.35\times$ vs.\ baseline $2.04\times$). However, Cheetah Run exhibits no reward gap ($1.00\times$) despite comparable CIVO severity ($8.27\times$ NVE); reward fidelity degradation is thus one transmission pathway but not a necessary condition. Direct value calibration confirms the critic internalizes this bias: the CIVO model's mean $V(s)/\text{MC-return}$ ratio reaches $7.80\times$ (median $3.40\times$; 100\% of sampled states overestimated) vs.\ median $1.05\times$ for the nearly-calibrated control (52.5\% overestimated). Causal ordering further supports a value-channel pathology: evaluation return collapses ${\sim}35$K steps before actor entropy rises ($+2.1$ nats/10K in collapsed configurations vs.\ $+0.07$--$0.22$ in healthy baselines; Appendix~\ref{sec:civo_mechanism}).

\paragraph{Temporal ordering ($n{=}2$).}
Time-series analysis across 12 dense checkpoints (10K--65K steps) provides evidence that reward fidelity degradation precedes value overestimation onset: the single-step fidelity ratio ($\text{MAE}_{\dt{=}2}/\text{MAE}_{\dt{=}1}$) crosses $1.1\times$ at 20K steps, while the value-to-eval ratio begins rising at ${\sim}$30K (reaching $1.59\times$ at 40K)---a ${\sim}$10K onset-to-onset lag ($r{=}0.81$, $p{=}0.002$; Figure~\ref{fig:temporal_ordering}). A second seed (exp\_066b) confirms the pattern: fidelity gap onset at ${\sim}$15K precedes VE onset at ${\sim}$24K (temporal lead ${\approx}$9K steps, consistent with seed~0's ${\approx}$10K), though seed~1's peak VE ($0.72\times$) remained below the $2.0\times$ threshold within 60K steps, which suggests seed-dependent progression rates. The gap arises from differential improvement: $\dt{=}1$ MAE decreases continuously ($0.093 \to 0.047$) while $\dt{=}2$ MAE plateaus (${\sim}0.08$; Appendix~\ref{sec:timeseries_fidelity}).

